1
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,126
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:38,303
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,383 --> 00:00:51,450
♪ ♪

4
00:01:04,998 --> 00:01:06,932
♪ ♪

5
00:01:58,552 --> 00:02:01,853
After you left your men to
investigate the murder scene,

6
00:02:01,922 --> 00:02:03,655
what did you do?

7
00:02:03,724 --> 00:02:06,758
A family had just been
slaughtered like animals.

8
00:02:06,827 --> 00:02:09,293
What the hell do
you think I did?

9
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:10,461
Your Honor...

10
00:02:10,531 --> 00:02:12,609
I went looking for
the man who did it.

11
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:14,399
You mean someone to pin it on?

12
00:02:14,467 --> 00:02:17,536
Why did you stop
Mr. Edmunds' automobile?

13
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:19,170
I wanted to talk to him.

14
00:02:19,239 --> 00:02:21,206
After he was
fired by Mr. Mailer,

15
00:02:21,274 --> 00:02:22,540
he was heard making threats

16
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:25,155
against him and his family.

17
00:02:25,179 --> 00:02:26,644
Come on, Captain Hanna.

18
00:02:26,713 --> 00:02:29,514
Did any witness place
my client at the scene?

19
00:02:29,583 --> 00:02:31,849
No, but... Was
there any evidence

20
00:02:31,918 --> 00:02:33,596
connecting my client
with the murders?

21
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,454
Not at that time, but...

22
00:02:35,522 --> 00:02:38,423
Did any reliable source
provide any information or tip?

23
00:02:38,492 --> 00:02:40,225
I don't rely on tips.

24
00:02:40,294 --> 00:02:42,627
So, with not one
shred of evidence,

25
00:02:42,696 --> 00:02:45,797
or anything remotely
resembling probable cause,

26
00:02:45,865 --> 00:02:49,101
you stopped my client,
and without his consent,

27
00:02:49,169 --> 00:02:51,503
searched both him
and his automobile?

28
00:02:51,572 --> 00:02:53,483
The murder weapon
was in the trunk.

29
00:02:53,507 --> 00:02:54,772
I had the guy cold.

30
00:02:54,841 --> 00:02:57,019
Your Honor, the
seizure of this weapon

31
00:02:57,043 --> 00:02:58,555
violated the fourth amendment

32
00:02:58,579 --> 00:03:00,045
of the Constitution.

33
00:03:00,113 --> 00:03:01,413
It must be suppressed.

34
00:03:01,481 --> 00:03:02,648
He's guilty as sin.

35
00:03:02,716 --> 00:03:04,716
You can't let him walk!

36
00:03:04,785 --> 00:03:07,419
Further, upon exclusion
of this evidence...

37
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:10,589
The only link between
my client and this crime...

38
00:03:10,658 --> 00:03:12,257
Defense moves that this case be

39
00:03:12,325 --> 00:03:14,325
dismissed for lack of evidence.

40
00:03:16,296 --> 00:03:18,129
In light of the flagrant abuse

41
00:03:18,198 --> 00:03:19,576
of the defendant's
constitutional rights,

42
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:23,234
I have no choice but
to dismiss this case.

43
00:03:26,673 --> 00:03:28,439
Hey, Edward? Ed Hanna.

44
00:03:28,508 --> 00:03:29,719
Hey! Ben.

45
00:03:29,743 --> 00:03:31,020
Ben, it's been too long.

46
00:03:31,044 --> 00:03:32,376
Yeah.

47
00:03:32,446 --> 00:03:35,247
What say we catch up over
T-bones at the steak house?

48
00:03:35,316 --> 00:03:37,060
We used to be
their best customers.

49
00:03:37,084 --> 00:03:38,324
Great idea. We'll do that soon.

50
00:03:38,385 --> 00:03:39,851
Um, I'll give you a call, Ben.

51
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:41,064
Is, uh...?

52
00:03:41,088 --> 00:03:42,287
Is everything all right?

53
00:03:42,356 --> 00:03:44,389
Oh, couldn't be
better. Just busy.

54
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:45,857
I understand that.

55
00:03:45,926 --> 00:03:47,492
Good to see you.

56
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:49,628
Right, Ben.

57
00:03:59,973 --> 00:04:01,740
Good wrap, buddy.
I can't believe it.

58
00:04:01,808 --> 00:04:04,053
Yeah, the good old
American way, huh?

59
00:04:04,077 --> 00:04:05,309
Take it easy, man.

60
00:04:05,379 --> 00:04:07,939
All right, see you, Bo.

61
00:04:18,491 --> 00:04:19,825
What?

62
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:46,730
Hey, get those people back.

63
00:04:46,754 --> 00:04:48,030
Come on.

64
00:04:48,054 --> 00:04:49,432
That's your good buddy.

65
00:04:49,456 --> 00:04:51,322
Hold on to your hat, honey.

66
00:04:51,391 --> 00:04:52,601
What do you got?

67
00:04:52,625 --> 00:04:54,125
Homicide some time last night.

68
00:04:54,194 --> 00:04:55,660
Coroner's on his way.

69
00:05:01,501 --> 00:05:03,568
What do you know?

70
00:05:03,637 --> 00:05:05,069
Just what I said.

71
00:05:05,139 --> 00:05:07,405
Single shot through the
base of the skull with this...

72
00:05:07,474 --> 00:05:09,107
.22, taped handle,

73
00:05:09,176 --> 00:05:10,909
no prints... no way to trace it.

74
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:12,255
Yeah, Mob hit.

75
00:05:12,279 --> 00:05:13,844
The Mob didn't do this.

76
00:05:13,913 --> 00:05:15,858
Look, when you've been
32 years on the force,

77
00:05:15,882 --> 00:05:18,161
you'll know a Mob
execution when you see one.

78
00:05:18,185 --> 00:05:19,517
This is how it's done.

79
00:05:19,586 --> 00:05:21,119
Edmunds wasn't Mob.

80
00:05:21,188 --> 00:05:24,122
And he didn't slaughter
the Mailer family, either.

81
00:05:24,191 --> 00:05:26,257
Hey, Shanley!

82
00:05:28,629 --> 00:05:32,130
I expect you to be
green, but not stupid.

83
00:05:32,199 --> 00:05:33,798
Last night, Frank Caliguire

84
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:36,100
walked into the
station and confessed.

85
00:05:36,170 --> 00:05:37,347
Caliguire's nothing.

86
00:05:37,371 --> 00:05:39,015
He's a punk. He's a punk

87
00:05:39,039 --> 00:05:41,106
high on PCP looking
for drug money.

88
00:05:41,175 --> 00:05:43,442
He tore up the Mailer
house and then killed them.

89
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:46,244
The PCP ran out, he crashed.

90
00:05:46,313 --> 00:05:48,980
He got scared, got
religion... who knows?

91
00:05:49,049 --> 00:05:50,514
But he gave himself up.

92
00:05:50,583 --> 00:05:52,350
I don't buy it.

93
00:05:52,419 --> 00:05:54,752
We had hard evidence on Edmunds.

94
00:05:54,821 --> 00:05:55,886
The gun.

95
00:05:55,955 --> 00:05:57,522
Caliguire said he planted it.

96
00:05:57,591 --> 00:05:59,023
Put a call in to the precinct.

97
00:05:59,092 --> 00:06:00,157
Morrison'll tell you.

98
00:06:00,227 --> 00:06:03,394
Edmunds was scum.

99
00:06:03,463 --> 00:06:06,430
His last conviction,
he assaulted a cop.

100
00:06:06,499 --> 00:06:08,066
Could've killed him.

101
00:06:08,135 --> 00:06:11,269
Whoever did Edmunds,
did this city a favor.

102
00:06:11,337 --> 00:06:14,505
Whoever did Edmunds
is guilty of murder.

103
00:06:14,575 --> 00:06:16,340
And I don't think
this is the first time

104
00:06:16,409 --> 00:06:17,709
this has happened.

105
00:06:17,778 --> 00:06:19,010
Meaning?

106
00:06:19,079 --> 00:06:21,646
I think you know.

107
00:06:21,714 --> 00:06:22,826
Shanley, let's bag
the rest of the evidence

108
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:24,730
Get it down to the lab. Come on.

109
00:06:33,059 --> 00:06:35,304
Listen, Jim. You've
got to stop calling

110
00:06:35,328 --> 00:06:37,240
Captain Hanna "The Mad Hatter."

111
00:06:37,264 --> 00:06:38,875
Stop telling people
he's flipped out.

112
00:06:38,899 --> 00:06:40,064
What do you call it, Reese?

113
00:06:40,133 --> 00:06:41,144
He's a good officer

114
00:06:41,168 --> 00:06:42,945
trying to keep
us alive out there.

115
00:06:42,969 --> 00:06:44,769
He's over the edge.

116
00:06:44,838 --> 00:06:45,903
He's a Section Eight.

117
00:06:45,972 --> 00:06:47,839
I'm telling you,
he's flipped out.

118
00:06:47,908 --> 00:06:49,507
I told you to can it!

119
00:07:11,398 --> 00:07:13,164
♪ ♪

120
00:07:13,233 --> 00:07:14,732
Take a hike, punk.

121
00:07:14,801 --> 00:07:16,579
Time to talk again, Malcolm.

122
00:07:16,603 --> 00:07:18,781
Man, forget it. Hey!

123
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:22,007
Right. Now!

124
00:07:44,831 --> 00:07:46,041
Hello, Captain.

125
00:07:46,065 --> 00:07:47,832
I'd like to talk to you.

126
00:07:51,438 --> 00:07:54,205
I've been thinking
about you, Shanley.

127
00:07:54,274 --> 00:07:56,741
You went to college,
didn't you? Captain...

128
00:07:56,810 --> 00:07:58,754
Maybe you should go
back, get your law degree.

129
00:07:58,778 --> 00:08:01,059
Start defending those
creeps you're so crazy about.

130
00:08:01,114 --> 00:08:04,615
Listen, Captain... ACLU's
the place for you, Shanley.

131
00:08:04,684 --> 00:08:06,617
Not my precinct.

132
00:08:06,686 --> 00:08:07,752
I'm a cop, Captain.

133
00:08:07,821 --> 00:08:09,232
Yeah, well, wait till
someone you know

134
00:08:09,256 --> 00:08:10,755
gets blown away.

135
00:08:10,824 --> 00:08:12,668
Let's see if you're still
screaming "due process."

136
00:08:12,692 --> 00:08:15,093
Everybody knows what
happened to your wife, Captain.

137
00:08:15,162 --> 00:08:16,994
In less than two years,

138
00:08:17,063 --> 00:08:19,103
five guys who slipped
through the system

139
00:08:19,166 --> 00:08:20,298
have been wasted.

140
00:08:20,366 --> 00:08:22,178
I think I found the connection.

141
00:08:22,202 --> 00:08:23,267
That a fact?

142
00:08:23,336 --> 00:08:24,602
In each case,

143
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:26,104
you were the arresting officer.

144
00:08:26,172 --> 00:08:27,883
I talked to a guy named Malcolm,

145
00:08:27,907 --> 00:08:30,274
he says he's done
business lately with a cop.

146
00:08:30,343 --> 00:08:31,421
Sounds like you're building

147
00:08:31,445 --> 00:08:33,478
a case against
somebody, Shanley.

148
00:08:33,547 --> 00:08:34,579
You got any proof?

149
00:08:34,647 --> 00:08:36,447
I don't have anything yet.

150
00:08:36,516 --> 00:08:38,816
And I don't want
to have anything.

151
00:08:38,885 --> 00:08:41,185
Captain, you've got 30 years.

152
00:08:41,254 --> 00:08:42,319
More decorations

153
00:08:42,388 --> 00:08:44,067
than most generals. Hell, you're

154
00:08:44,091 --> 00:08:45,211
a legend at the academy:

155
00:08:45,258 --> 00:08:47,492
Edward Hanna, the cop's cop.

156
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:50,428
What do you want
from me, Shanley?

157
00:08:50,496 --> 00:08:52,430
Retire, Captain.

158
00:08:52,499 --> 00:08:54,032
Pack it in.

159
00:08:55,369 --> 00:08:57,301
I'm afraid I can't do that.

160
00:08:57,371 --> 00:08:59,382
I want you to know
I'm investigating this.

161
00:08:59,406 --> 00:09:00,616
And sooner or later, I'll have

162
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,473
to go to Internal Affairs.

163
00:09:06,947 --> 00:09:08,858
Retirement's no
dishonor, Captain.

164
00:09:08,882 --> 00:09:10,548
Think about it.

165
00:09:18,659 --> 00:09:20,591
♪ ♪

166
00:10:11,444 --> 00:10:13,378
♪ ♪

167
00:10:58,491 --> 00:11:00,424
Who is it? Edward Hanna.

168
00:11:06,166 --> 00:11:07,576
I've been thinking
about what you said

169
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:08,878
about retirement.

170
00:11:08,902 --> 00:11:11,014
It's not the worst
idea I ever heard.

171
00:11:11,038 --> 00:11:12,203
That's a relief, Captain.

172
00:11:12,272 --> 00:11:14,605
And I'll pack it in.

173
00:11:14,675 --> 00:11:16,941
Let some hotshot
like you take over.

174
00:11:18,244 --> 00:11:20,078
Now, you get over here
to the phone. Come on.

175
00:11:26,853 --> 00:11:33,390
Now... You dial
5-5-5... 5-8-1-3.

176
00:11:33,459 --> 00:11:36,005
That'll be Sergeant Reese.

177
00:11:36,029 --> 00:11:38,269
You tell him to meet you
in the Pike Street alley

178
00:11:38,298 --> 00:11:39,730
in 20 minutes.

179
00:11:39,799 --> 00:11:41,132
Dial.

180
00:11:49,776 --> 00:11:51,709
Stop right here.

181
00:11:52,779 --> 00:11:56,147
Look... I wrote everything down.

182
00:11:56,216 --> 00:11:57,826
Something happens to
me, they're going to find

183
00:11:57,850 --> 00:11:59,395
my notes and know you did it.

184
00:11:59,419 --> 00:12:01,352
Shut up and turn around.

185
00:12:01,421 --> 00:12:03,799
You'll never get
away with it. Come on!

186
00:12:15,402 --> 00:12:16,834
Shanley!

187
00:12:23,243 --> 00:12:25,843
Shanley, oh, God!

188
00:12:32,652 --> 00:12:34,519
♪ ♪

189
00:13:03,983 --> 00:13:05,917
♪ ♪

190
00:13:48,494 --> 00:13:50,494
Freeze it right there, buddy.

191
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:54,198
Get up real slow.

192
00:13:58,704 --> 00:14:01,706
For the twentieth
time, he called, I went.

193
00:14:01,774 --> 00:14:02,940
I heard a shot.

194
00:14:03,008 --> 00:14:05,776
I investigated I got clobbered.

195
00:14:07,647 --> 00:14:10,059
Why'd you give Shanley $5,000?

196
00:14:10,083 --> 00:14:11,149
I didn't!

197
00:14:11,217 --> 00:14:12,461
What were you doing
with a quarter pound

198
00:14:12,485 --> 00:14:13,525
of coke in your car?

199
00:14:15,655 --> 00:14:17,721
I don't know.

200
00:14:29,669 --> 00:14:32,570
Anything? Still sticking
to his story like glue.

201
00:14:32,639 --> 00:14:34,316
Well, then, damn it,
he's telling the truth.

202
00:14:34,340 --> 00:14:36,552
Shanley, Reese...
both good cops.

203
00:14:36,576 --> 00:14:38,654
I can't believe they'd
be dealing in drugs.

204
00:14:38,678 --> 00:14:39,678
What about the guy

205
00:14:39,713 --> 00:14:41,412
in the red sweatshirt
seen in the alley?

206
00:14:41,481 --> 00:14:42,658
I'll find him.

207
00:14:42,682 --> 00:14:43,802
Just up from Ballistics.

208
00:14:43,850 --> 00:14:45,282
Eh, thanks.

209
00:14:48,287 --> 00:14:50,621
Ah... The bullet
that killed Shanley

210
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:52,723
came from Reese's gun.

211
00:14:52,792 --> 00:14:53,957
Damn it.

212
00:14:54,026 --> 00:14:55,460
Book him.

213
00:15:07,606 --> 00:15:09,140
When I came to,

214
00:15:09,208 --> 00:15:11,053
two officers were
standing over me

215
00:15:11,077 --> 00:15:12,943
and Officer Shanley was

216
00:15:13,012 --> 00:15:15,612
lying there... dead.

217
00:15:17,583 --> 00:15:19,316
I know how it looks.

218
00:15:19,385 --> 00:15:21,585
But I didn't kill him.

219
00:15:23,322 --> 00:15:25,689
I guess they took your
picture when they booked you?

220
00:15:25,758 --> 00:15:28,292
Yeah, it's standard procedure.

221
00:15:28,361 --> 00:15:30,261
We'll have a record

222
00:15:30,329 --> 00:15:32,408
of that bruise on your neck.

223
00:15:32,432 --> 00:15:34,343
How did you and Officer
Shanley get along?

224
00:15:34,367 --> 00:15:36,100
You're not going to like this.

225
00:15:36,169 --> 00:15:38,636
I thought Shanley was a jerk.

226
00:15:38,705 --> 00:15:40,204
He was always blowing off

227
00:15:40,273 --> 00:15:41,505
about Captain Hanna,

228
00:15:41,574 --> 00:15:43,118
calling him "The Mad Hatter."

229
00:15:43,142 --> 00:15:45,409
"Mad Hatter"?

230
00:15:45,478 --> 00:15:47,445
Captain Hanna's a good cop.

231
00:15:47,514 --> 00:15:49,647
Okay, he may be
a little hard-nosed.

232
00:15:51,150 --> 00:15:53,384
Try a lot hard-nosed.

233
00:15:53,452 --> 00:15:54,685
You know him?

234
00:15:54,754 --> 00:15:57,522
Since my hair was brown
and his was a crew cut.

235
00:15:57,590 --> 00:15:59,624
Long time.

236
00:15:59,692 --> 00:16:00,970
Officer Shanley's partner,

237
00:16:00,994 --> 00:16:02,505
what can you tell me about him?

238
00:16:02,529 --> 00:16:03,661
Her, Mr. Matlock.

239
00:16:03,730 --> 00:16:05,829
Donna Dawson's a good cop.

240
00:16:05,898 --> 00:16:07,343
Clearheaded, quick on her feet.

241
00:16:07,367 --> 00:16:08,966
And loyal.

242
00:16:09,034 --> 00:16:10,434
Especially to Shanley.

243
00:16:18,978 --> 00:16:20,578
Officer Dawson!

244
00:16:21,814 --> 00:16:24,060
Hi, I'm, uh, Ben Matlock,

245
00:16:24,084 --> 00:16:26,184
Jerry Reese's attorney.

246
00:16:26,252 --> 00:16:28,752
My partner wasn't
dirty, Mr. Matlock.

247
00:16:29,989 --> 00:16:32,301
Uh, then you
don't buy the theory

248
00:16:32,325 --> 00:16:34,470
that, uh, he and,
uh, Jerry Reese

249
00:16:34,494 --> 00:16:36,760
were dealing drugs? Never.

250
00:16:36,829 --> 00:16:38,463
Jim was set up.

251
00:16:38,531 --> 00:16:40,164
And when I find out who and why,

252
00:16:40,233 --> 00:16:41,873
then I'll know why
Jerry Reese killed him.

253
00:16:41,935 --> 00:16:45,336
Uh, you don't think,
uh, someone else

254
00:16:45,405 --> 00:16:48,039
set up Jerry Reese as well?

255
00:16:49,242 --> 00:16:50,841
Look, Mr. Matlock,

256
00:16:50,910 --> 00:16:52,554
Jerry Reese was
found at the scene,

257
00:16:52,578 --> 00:16:54,390
and his gun was
the murder weapon.

258
00:16:54,414 --> 00:16:56,713
The only thing I'm
missing is motive.

259
00:16:56,782 --> 00:16:58,716
And I'm digging.

260
00:17:00,953 --> 00:17:02,953
Maybe we could work together.

261
00:17:03,022 --> 00:17:04,955
You gotta be kidding.

262
00:17:06,025 --> 00:17:07,892
I've known a lot of people.

263
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:10,895
But there was only one
person I would trust my life with.

264
00:17:10,963 --> 00:17:13,297
And did, every day.

265
00:17:13,365 --> 00:17:15,065
That was Jim Shanley.

266
00:17:15,134 --> 00:17:16,500
Now, if Jerry Reese killed him,

267
00:17:16,569 --> 00:17:18,502
why in hell would I help you?

268
00:17:20,139 --> 00:17:22,739
Well, I'll tell you.

269
00:17:25,477 --> 00:17:27,078
In the first place,

270
00:17:27,146 --> 00:17:30,081
Jerry Reese didn't
kill your partner.

271
00:17:30,149 --> 00:17:32,483
And I intend to prove that.

272
00:17:32,552 --> 00:17:35,919
And when I do prove it,

273
00:17:35,989 --> 00:17:38,922
it'll clear Jerry Reese,

274
00:17:38,991 --> 00:17:41,792
and it'll clear Jim
Shanley's name as well.

275
00:17:59,812 --> 00:18:02,579
Jim is investigating
our precinct captain.

276
00:18:03,949 --> 00:18:06,250
Edward Hanna?

277
00:18:06,319 --> 00:18:08,719
Captain Hanna's wife was
murdered by some punk on parole.

278
00:18:08,788 --> 00:18:12,055
And since then, the
captain's been... difficult.

279
00:18:12,124 --> 00:18:15,826
Well, he had a terrible
thing happen to him.

280
00:18:15,895 --> 00:18:17,673
But the captain's
pulled some stuff.

281
00:18:17,697 --> 00:18:19,630
It's gone way past bizarre.

282
00:18:19,699 --> 00:18:22,259
And Jim was keeping a list.

283
00:18:24,136 --> 00:18:27,404
I've known Edward
Hanna for 30 years.

284
00:18:27,473 --> 00:18:30,341
Jim told me that when he
was through investigating

285
00:18:30,410 --> 00:18:33,177
Captain Hanna, he'd be
going to the review board,

286
00:18:33,245 --> 00:18:35,805
maybe even Internal Affairs.

287
00:18:42,988 --> 00:18:45,000
Do you have any
idea where your partner

288
00:18:45,024 --> 00:18:47,191
was keeping this list?

289
00:19:06,445 --> 00:19:08,390
You want to know the names

290
00:19:08,414 --> 00:19:10,092
of the most attractive
women in Atlanta?

291
00:19:10,116 --> 00:19:11,949
Right here.

292
00:19:12,018 --> 00:19:13,617
Shanley dated them all.

293
00:19:13,686 --> 00:19:14,964
I figured if he

294
00:19:14,988 --> 00:19:17,821
was dealing, he
might've kept a ledger.

295
00:19:17,890 --> 00:19:19,456
Something in here.

296
00:19:19,525 --> 00:19:20,590
Any luck?

297
00:19:20,659 --> 00:19:22,159
Oh, nothing.

298
00:19:22,227 --> 00:19:23,360
Call just came in.

299
00:19:23,429 --> 00:19:24,773
One of our men
talked to somebody

300
00:19:24,797 --> 00:19:26,541
and said they saw a guy in a red

301
00:19:26,565 --> 00:19:27,605
sweatshirt sleeping it off

302
00:19:27,667 --> 00:19:28,907
behind a Dumpster this morning.

303
00:19:28,935 --> 00:19:30,167
Where?

304
00:19:30,236 --> 00:19:32,135
700 block of Pike.

305
00:19:32,204 --> 00:19:34,905
Here, do something with this.

306
00:19:44,183 --> 00:19:45,849
Hey, man!

307
00:19:45,918 --> 00:19:46,928
Game's over.

308
00:19:46,952 --> 00:19:48,552
Who's next?!

309
00:19:49,688 --> 00:19:50,968
Malcolm, old buddy.

310
00:19:51,024 --> 00:19:52,701
I want you to meet
a friend of mine,

311
00:19:52,725 --> 00:19:55,292
Ben Matlock. How you doing?

312
00:19:55,361 --> 00:19:56,860
He's aka "Alice."

313
00:19:56,929 --> 00:19:58,707
Short for Alice's Restaurant.

314
00:19:58,731 --> 00:20:00,976
Anything you
need, Alice can get.

315
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,035
Fake birth certificates,
new driver's license,

316
00:20:04,103 --> 00:20:07,571
hot cars, untraceable firearms.

317
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:09,640
I don't know what
you're talking about.

318
00:20:09,675 --> 00:20:11,320
Jim Shanley was
in here yesterday.

319
00:20:11,344 --> 00:20:13,377
What did he want?

320
00:20:13,446 --> 00:20:14,845
Must have missed him.

321
00:20:14,914 --> 00:20:17,474
Been downtown lately, Alice?

322
00:20:19,085 --> 00:20:20,551
Go ahead.

323
00:20:20,619 --> 00:20:23,453
I'd rather be rousted than
wind up dead in some alley.

324
00:20:23,522 --> 00:20:26,957
Are you saying
that Officer Shanley

325
00:20:27,026 --> 00:20:29,960
was killed because of
something you told him?

326
00:20:30,029 --> 00:20:32,062
I'm saying nothing.

327
00:20:32,131 --> 00:20:33,463
It was guns, wasn't it?

328
00:20:33,532 --> 00:20:35,276
He wanted to find
out who's been getting

329
00:20:35,300 --> 00:20:36,667
cold .22s off of you.

330
00:20:36,736 --> 00:20:38,480
What did you tell
him? That I'm lousy

331
00:20:38,504 --> 00:20:40,215
at remembering names.
How about the name

332
00:20:40,239 --> 00:20:41,239
Edward Hanna?

333
00:20:44,677 --> 00:20:48,979
Look... Ain't no way

334
00:20:49,048 --> 00:20:51,982
is anyone going to
get a name out of me.

335
00:20:52,051 --> 00:20:54,818
Being out of
business is one thing.

336
00:20:54,887 --> 00:20:56,086
Being dead is another.

337
00:21:13,005 --> 00:21:15,572
I'm looking for a guy
wearing a red sweatshirt.

338
00:21:15,641 --> 00:21:17,961
You know, the kind with a
hood... Alley red... a boozer.

339
00:21:18,010 --> 00:21:19,509
Have you seen him around here?

340
00:21:19,578 --> 00:21:20,845
Nope.

341
00:21:20,913 --> 00:21:22,157
Don't walk away
from me! Hey, let go!

342
00:21:22,181 --> 00:21:24,014
Think about it, hair-brain!

343
00:21:24,083 --> 00:21:26,183
A guy with a red
sweatshirt, a boozer.

344
00:21:26,252 --> 00:21:27,462
Now, where have you seen him?!

345
00:21:27,486 --> 00:21:29,097
I-I... Have you seen him
anywhere around here?!

346
00:21:29,121 --> 00:21:31,481
No, I haven't, man! I
swear. I swear. Ah, sh...!

347
00:21:37,697 --> 00:21:39,596
I heard you were down here.

348
00:21:39,665 --> 00:21:41,143
Ben.

349
00:21:41,167 --> 00:21:43,266
Weren't you a
little rough on him.

350
00:21:43,335 --> 00:21:44,646
Should've been a lot rougher.

351
00:21:44,670 --> 00:21:46,136
Things have changed, Ben.

352
00:21:46,205 --> 00:21:49,306
Punks carry Uzis
instead of switchblades.

353
00:21:49,374 --> 00:21:52,442
Crack is cheap.
Life is a lot cheaper.

354
00:21:52,511 --> 00:21:53,977
You let down for a minute,

355
00:21:54,046 --> 00:21:56,112
they're playing
"Taps" over your coffin.

356
00:21:57,450 --> 00:21:59,516
Life hasn't improved.

357
00:21:59,585 --> 00:22:03,120
Of course, you can't
take care of it by yourself.

358
00:22:03,188 --> 00:22:04,988
Why are you

359
00:22:05,058 --> 00:22:07,891
conducting this
investigation personally?

360
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:09,771
Well, when you want
something done right, Ben,

361
00:22:09,795 --> 00:22:10,827
you do it yourself.

362
00:22:10,896 --> 00:22:14,097
I want to thank you
for taking on Reese.

363
00:22:14,166 --> 00:22:15,666
You know, he's a good man.

364
00:22:15,734 --> 00:22:17,378
I don't know what
the hell happened.

365
00:22:17,402 --> 00:22:20,571
How did, uh, Officer
Shanley measure up?

366
00:22:20,639 --> 00:22:24,908
He made his share of mistakes,
but he showed a lot of promise.

367
00:22:24,977 --> 00:22:27,978
Do you know, uh...

368
00:22:28,046 --> 00:22:32,449
the talk was that,
uh, he didn't like you.

369
00:22:32,518 --> 00:22:36,887
He called you "The Mad Hatter."

370
00:22:38,191 --> 00:22:41,725
He kept some kind of crazy list

371
00:22:41,794 --> 00:22:43,360
with things you had done

372
00:22:43,429 --> 00:22:45,629
and he was telling people

373
00:22:45,698 --> 00:22:48,532
that you ought to
resign from the force.

374
00:22:49,635 --> 00:22:51,268
I'm not surprised.

375
00:22:51,337 --> 00:22:53,148
When a person
shows a lot of potential,

376
00:22:53,172 --> 00:22:55,072
I'm a lot tougher on them.

377
00:22:55,141 --> 00:22:57,608
But as far as his
writing down stuff,

378
00:22:57,677 --> 00:22:59,955
believe me, he had
better things to do.

379
00:22:59,979 --> 00:23:01,278
I kept that kid hopping.

380
00:23:01,347 --> 00:23:03,907
Look, I've got a
lot of work to do.

381
00:23:03,950 --> 00:23:05,226
I'll call you next week, okay?

382
00:23:05,250 --> 00:23:06,728
Okay, Ed. Take care, Ben.

383
00:23:06,752 --> 00:23:08,151
Okay.

384
00:23:54,133 --> 00:23:56,366
♪ ♪

385
00:25:48,247 --> 00:25:50,047
Freeze.

386
00:25:50,115 --> 00:25:52,649
Don't move.

387
00:25:54,052 --> 00:25:54,985
Officer Dawson.

388
00:25:55,053 --> 00:25:56,598
Cops can see in the dark,

389
00:25:56,622 --> 00:25:58,500
Mr. Matlock, but lawyers...

390
00:25:58,524 --> 00:26:02,626
Well, the lights were on when...

391
00:26:29,555 --> 00:26:30,788
Hmm.

392
00:26:30,856 --> 00:26:33,657
Well, I could have
sworn I heard somebody.

393
00:26:33,726 --> 00:26:35,792
You did.

394
00:26:46,672 --> 00:26:49,039
They treating you all right?

395
00:26:49,108 --> 00:26:51,341
Nobody looks me in the eye.

396
00:26:51,409 --> 00:26:56,346
Well, a cop accused
of killing a cop...

397
00:26:56,414 --> 00:26:57,680
I'm okay.

398
00:26:57,750 --> 00:26:59,716
Good.

399
00:26:59,785 --> 00:27:04,221
Now... your gun
killed Jim Shanley

400
00:27:04,289 --> 00:27:06,522
but you didn't?

401
00:27:06,591 --> 00:27:08,302
Who could have
switched guns with you?

402
00:27:08,326 --> 00:27:10,994
My gun is either
on me or locked up.

403
00:27:11,063 --> 00:27:12,562
When is it locked up?

404
00:27:12,632 --> 00:27:14,498
When I shower at the precinct

405
00:27:14,566 --> 00:27:15,799
or when I work out.

406
00:27:15,868 --> 00:27:17,001
Then it's in my locker.

407
00:27:17,069 --> 00:27:21,371
Did you work out the day
Jim Shanley was killed?

408
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:23,607
Every day, after my shift.

409
00:27:23,676 --> 00:27:25,854
Is there a master
key to those lockers?

410
00:27:25,878 --> 00:27:28,245
Sure, in the office.

411
00:27:28,313 --> 00:27:31,815
You mean anybody in that office

412
00:27:31,884 --> 00:27:34,985
could've opened your locker
while you were working out

413
00:27:35,054 --> 00:27:36,531
and switched guns with you?

414
00:27:36,555 --> 00:27:39,289
Anybody authorized
to go in the office

415
00:27:39,358 --> 00:27:40,557
can go in any locker.

416
00:27:40,625 --> 00:27:41,691
That's procedure.

417
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:43,259
Oh.

418
00:27:43,328 --> 00:27:46,463
Are you trying to
say another cop?

419
00:27:46,532 --> 00:27:48,643
You don't think that's possible?

420
00:27:48,667 --> 00:27:50,099
Hell no.

421
00:27:50,168 --> 00:27:53,703
Why would...? No,
it's not possible.

422
00:27:53,772 --> 00:27:56,706
Hmm.

423
00:27:56,775 --> 00:27:59,709
I looked through your
belongings in the property room.

424
00:27:59,778 --> 00:28:02,846
I noticed you had
a St. Jude medal.

425
00:28:02,915 --> 00:28:07,317
I saw one exactly like it
in Jim Shanley's effects.

426
00:28:07,385 --> 00:28:09,597
Captain Hanna gave them
to everybody one Christmas.

427
00:28:09,621 --> 00:28:12,055
It was his wife's idea.

428
00:28:12,123 --> 00:28:15,058
She should have
given one to herself.

429
00:28:18,430 --> 00:28:20,197
Mr. Matlock.

430
00:28:20,265 --> 00:28:22,477
I was just going to call you.

431
00:28:22,501 --> 00:28:25,101
I did some investigating.

432
00:28:27,005 --> 00:28:29,384
Five suspects freed
on technicalities

433
00:28:29,408 --> 00:28:31,620
wound up shot to
death a short time later.

434
00:28:31,644 --> 00:28:35,846
Captain Hanna was
involved in all five cases.

435
00:28:35,915 --> 00:28:39,382
And, just this week, a guy named
Bo Edmunds was found dead

436
00:28:39,451 --> 00:28:42,552
the day after a murder case
against him was dismissed.

437
00:28:42,620 --> 00:28:44,855
Know why it was dismissed?

438
00:28:44,924 --> 00:28:47,390
No. Captain Hanna performed

439
00:28:47,459 --> 00:28:49,459
an illegal search and seizure.

440
00:28:58,203 --> 00:29:00,437
Hey.

441
00:29:00,506 --> 00:29:02,005
Hey, Ben.

442
00:29:02,074 --> 00:29:03,673
How's uh, how's Jerry Reese?

443
00:29:03,742 --> 00:29:06,109
Oh, he's-he's holding on.

444
00:29:06,178 --> 00:29:08,211
Did you find the man
in the red sweatshirt?

445
00:29:08,280 --> 00:29:10,180
Oh, just a matter of time.

446
00:29:10,249 --> 00:29:11,460
How about you?

447
00:29:11,484 --> 00:29:13,183
You getting anywhere?

448
00:29:13,251 --> 00:29:16,452
You know, I was thinking
on the way over here

449
00:29:16,521 --> 00:29:18,755
about how we met.

450
00:29:18,824 --> 00:29:21,758
Yeah, I was just a
rookie, and you were...

451
00:29:21,827 --> 00:29:23,860
Scared out of my shoes.

452
00:29:23,929 --> 00:29:24,961
My first case.

453
00:29:25,030 --> 00:29:26,463
You won it.

454
00:29:26,532 --> 00:29:28,631
Your testimony didn't hurt.

455
00:29:28,701 --> 00:29:32,835
You were the best
rookie I ever saw.

456
00:29:32,904 --> 00:29:35,505
And the best cop.

457
00:29:35,574 --> 00:29:38,408
Well, I believe in justice.

458
00:29:38,477 --> 00:29:40,243
So do you.

459
00:29:40,312 --> 00:29:43,179
That's why we're friends.

460
00:29:45,517 --> 00:29:48,451
Uh... I've got a theory.

461
00:29:48,520 --> 00:29:50,386
You want to hear it?

462
00:29:50,455 --> 00:29:51,755
Yeah.

463
00:29:51,824 --> 00:29:54,324
Shoot.

464
00:29:54,393 --> 00:29:56,626
Okay, here it is.

465
00:29:58,998 --> 00:30:04,567
Now, Jim Shanley was
killed by another officer,

466
00:30:04,636 --> 00:30:08,104
that's a fact, but
not Jerry Reese.

467
00:30:08,174 --> 00:30:11,307
A third officer was involved.

468
00:30:13,412 --> 00:30:16,146
What makes you
think it was a cop?

469
00:30:16,215 --> 00:30:18,175
Because of the
bruises on his neck.

470
00:30:18,217 --> 00:30:21,751
They were made by
a police choke hold.

471
00:30:21,820 --> 00:30:24,254
And that patrol car
that just happened by.

472
00:30:24,323 --> 00:30:26,889
Hell, the killer knew that
patrol car was coming by

473
00:30:26,958 --> 00:30:30,727
because the killer was a cop.

474
00:30:30,796 --> 00:30:31,796
Interesting theory, huh?

475
00:30:31,863 --> 00:30:33,129
Yeah.

476
00:30:33,198 --> 00:30:36,600
I'll tell ya, why don't you
keep me posted, huh?

477
00:30:36,669 --> 00:30:37,669
Yeah.

478
00:30:37,702 --> 00:30:38,634
Oh!

479
00:30:38,703 --> 00:30:41,263
Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh.

480
00:30:41,306 --> 00:30:46,643
Did you know that Jim Shanley
was keeping a Mad Hatter list?

481
00:30:46,711 --> 00:30:49,846
I found some notes
scribbled on a pad

482
00:30:49,915 --> 00:30:51,547
in his house the other night.

483
00:30:51,616 --> 00:30:53,561
Did you really do
all those things?

484
00:30:53,585 --> 00:30:57,087
I don't know. I
never saw the list.

485
00:30:57,155 --> 00:30:58,755
Oh, here's a copy.

486
00:31:09,501 --> 00:31:11,968
This it?

487
00:31:12,037 --> 00:31:12,969
Yeah.

488
00:31:13,038 --> 00:31:14,871
The way this is written,

489
00:31:14,940 --> 00:31:17,174
it makes me out a nut case.

490
00:31:17,242 --> 00:31:19,675
I can't figure Jim Shanley.

491
00:31:19,745 --> 00:31:21,711
Keep it, keep it, keep it.

492
00:31:21,780 --> 00:31:23,312
I'll see ya.

493
00:31:25,083 --> 00:31:26,582
Take care, Ben.

494
00:31:31,423 --> 00:31:33,423
I'm looking for a guy
wearing a red sweatshirt.

495
00:31:33,491 --> 00:31:35,392
You know, the kind
with a hood... alley red.

496
00:31:35,460 --> 00:31:37,593
Any of you gals seen a
guy like that around here?

497
00:31:37,662 --> 00:31:40,096
If you see him, let
me know, all right?

498
00:31:40,165 --> 00:31:42,344
Get away from me! No,
come on, work with me.

499
00:31:42,368 --> 00:31:44,445
You still looking for that
guy in the red sweatshirt?

500
00:31:44,469 --> 00:31:45,469
Yeah, have you seen him?

501
00:31:45,537 --> 00:31:47,782
Yeah, in an alley on St. Claire

502
00:31:47,806 --> 00:31:49,505
between Seventh and Eighth.

503
00:31:49,574 --> 00:31:51,286
Yeah? He was
looking for breakfast.

504
00:31:51,310 --> 00:31:54,911
Wait. Hey, what's
in it for me, huh?

505
00:32:04,256 --> 00:32:05,555
Son of a...

506
00:32:21,740 --> 00:32:24,540
Stop, stop.

507
00:32:53,271 --> 00:32:55,705
♪ ♪

508
00:33:26,438 --> 00:33:27,770
Freeze!

509
00:33:27,839 --> 00:33:29,272
Don't shoot!

510
00:33:36,814 --> 00:33:39,749
I wouldn't do that, Edward.

511
00:33:44,189 --> 00:33:46,957
I didn't want to believe it.

512
00:33:47,025 --> 00:33:49,926
You were going to
shoot him, Edward.

513
00:33:49,995 --> 00:33:51,138
Because you were afraid

514
00:33:51,162 --> 00:33:54,431
he could identify you.

515
00:33:54,499 --> 00:33:58,802
He never saw Officer
Shanley murdered.

516
00:33:58,871 --> 00:34:00,236
He never saw you.

517
00:34:00,305 --> 00:34:03,840
He ran because he heard a siren.

518
00:34:03,908 --> 00:34:06,209
People who sleep
behind Dumpsters

519
00:34:06,277 --> 00:34:09,779
don't think of policemen
as their friends.

520
00:34:09,848 --> 00:34:13,817
I don't presume to know what
goes on in your head, Ben,

521
00:34:13,885 --> 00:34:16,219
other than getting
your client off.

522
00:34:16,287 --> 00:34:20,156
I was merely pursuing a
witness in a murder case.

523
00:34:20,225 --> 00:34:24,961
And now that you've
determined he's not a witness...

524
00:34:27,131 --> 00:34:30,132
we can all go home.

525
00:34:56,661 --> 00:35:01,264
Jim Shanley, Bo Edmunds,

526
00:35:01,333 --> 00:35:07,437
Tavares, Taylor,
Hill and Fremont.

527
00:35:07,506 --> 00:35:11,474
You killed them all.

528
00:35:13,278 --> 00:35:18,915
Ben, think about
what you're saying.

529
00:35:18,984 --> 00:35:21,584
Edward Hanna,

530
00:35:21,653 --> 00:35:26,022
captain of police,
guilty of murder?

531
00:35:26,091 --> 00:35:29,692
This is Sergeant Shanley's list.

532
00:35:29,761 --> 00:35:31,228
I saw the list.

533
00:35:31,296 --> 00:35:32,729
It's nothing.

534
00:35:34,533 --> 00:35:36,699
It'll do for openers.

535
00:35:40,037 --> 00:35:41,971
And I have evidence.

536
00:35:42,040 --> 00:35:45,008
What kind of evidence?

537
00:35:45,076 --> 00:35:46,977
You'll hear it in court.

538
00:35:47,045 --> 00:35:50,246
You'll tell me now.

539
00:35:50,315 --> 00:35:53,015
Sorry.

540
00:35:53,084 --> 00:35:59,022
Ben... don't count
on our friendship.

541
00:35:59,091 --> 00:36:01,157
Edward...

542
00:36:03,428 --> 00:36:06,162
I don't know you.

543
00:36:07,933 --> 00:36:09,532
Where's your medal?

544
00:36:09,601 --> 00:36:11,334
What medal?

545
00:36:11,402 --> 00:36:14,838
Your St. Jude medal
your wife gave you.

546
00:36:14,906 --> 00:36:16,172
At home in a drawer.

547
00:36:16,241 --> 00:36:21,077
Or maybe Sergeant
Shanley put up a struggle,

548
00:36:21,145 --> 00:36:22,778
tore it off.

549
00:36:22,847 --> 00:36:28,417
Maybe it's still
somewhere in the alley.

550
00:36:32,090 --> 00:36:33,701
Detain him! Barnes, Drucker!

551
00:36:33,725 --> 00:36:34,735
Captain, you want us...?

552
00:36:34,759 --> 00:36:36,039
You put him in

553
00:36:36,094 --> 00:36:37,971
a holding room and don't
let him out of your sight.

554
00:36:37,995 --> 00:36:39,029
You want us to...?

555
00:36:39,097 --> 00:36:41,964
Are you questioning
my order, officer?

556
00:36:42,034 --> 00:36:43,799
Sorry, Mr. Matlock.

557
00:36:45,037 --> 00:36:46,680
What the hell are
you gawking at?

558
00:36:46,704 --> 00:36:48,037
Go on!

559
00:37:20,305 --> 00:37:23,106
My name is Captain
Edward Calvin Hanna.

560
00:37:23,175 --> 00:37:25,809
I work for the Atlanta
police department.

561
00:37:25,877 --> 00:37:31,147
I reside at 645 Biloxi
Street in Atlanta, Georgia.

562
00:37:31,216 --> 00:37:36,119
How long have you been a
policeman, Captain Hanna?

563
00:37:36,188 --> 00:37:37,520
32 years.

564
00:37:37,588 --> 00:37:40,290
And you've been
a captain for...?

565
00:37:40,358 --> 00:37:41,435
Ten years.

566
00:37:41,459 --> 00:37:44,861
And in that time,
you have acquired

567
00:37:44,930 --> 00:37:49,265
a most impressive reputation.

568
00:37:49,334 --> 00:37:54,704
I believe they call
you the "cop's cop."

569
00:37:54,773 --> 00:37:56,139
I've heard that.

570
00:37:56,207 --> 00:37:58,908
And in your 32 years,

571
00:37:58,977 --> 00:38:02,411
you've received dozes
of citizen commendations.

572
00:38:02,481 --> 00:38:05,381
You've been awarded
the Police Star,

573
00:38:05,450 --> 00:38:09,285
you're the only
officer on the APD

574
00:38:09,354 --> 00:38:12,722
to have twice received
the Medal of Valor.

575
00:38:12,791 --> 00:38:14,957
Any officer in the world

576
00:38:15,026 --> 00:38:16,693
would be proud of your record.

577
00:38:16,762 --> 00:38:18,461
I am.

578
00:38:21,900 --> 00:38:28,104
Some cases are harder
than others, Captain Hanna.

579
00:38:28,172 --> 00:38:32,842
But of course, you know that.

580
00:38:32,911 --> 00:38:38,380
When did the people
who work under you

581
00:38:38,449 --> 00:38:41,818
start calling you
"The Mad Hatter"?

582
00:38:43,888 --> 00:38:45,833
Nobody's ever called
me that to my face,

583
00:38:45,857 --> 00:38:48,190
so, uh, I wouldn't know.

584
00:38:48,259 --> 00:38:50,271
You know where the
nickname came from?

585
00:38:50,295 --> 00:38:52,195
No idea.

586
00:38:52,264 --> 00:38:54,797
I might.

587
00:38:54,866 --> 00:38:59,168
The decedent, James
Shanley, wrote it down.

588
00:38:59,237 --> 00:39:00,436
Objection:

589
00:39:00,505 --> 00:39:04,340
Mr. Matlock is testifying,
not asking questions.

590
00:39:04,408 --> 00:39:07,010
I seriously doubt the
relevancy of any of this.

591
00:39:07,078 --> 00:39:08,244
Goes to motive, Your Honor.

592
00:39:08,312 --> 00:39:10,713
I certainly hope
so, Mr. Matlock.

593
00:39:10,781 --> 00:39:11,881
Proceed.

594
00:39:11,949 --> 00:39:14,117
Thank you.

595
00:39:14,185 --> 00:39:17,620
When a patrol officer lost

596
00:39:17,688 --> 00:39:22,325
his Atlanta Police Department
baseball cap a couple years ago,

597
00:39:22,394 --> 00:39:27,229
you suspended him for
three weeks without pay.

598
00:39:27,298 --> 00:39:28,965
That is indeed true.

599
00:39:29,033 --> 00:39:31,479
Three weeks without pay.

600
00:39:31,503 --> 00:39:32,780
Officer Shanley

601
00:39:32,804 --> 00:39:35,070
thought that was
fairly irrational,

602
00:39:35,139 --> 00:39:38,007
so he started calling
you "The Mad Hatter."

603
00:39:38,076 --> 00:39:41,511
There was no irrationality.

604
00:39:41,580 --> 00:39:43,591
The emblem of the
Atlanta Police Department

605
00:39:43,615 --> 00:39:44,814
is on our baseball caps.

606
00:39:44,882 --> 00:39:47,250
It looks like a badge.

607
00:39:47,319 --> 00:39:50,920
Anyone wearing one would be
presumed to be a police officer.

608
00:39:50,988 --> 00:39:53,823
If a street punk got
his hands on one,

609
00:39:53,891 --> 00:39:57,359
he could pass
himself off as a cop.

610
00:39:57,428 --> 00:39:58,572
Anything could happen.

611
00:39:58,596 --> 00:40:03,066
By severely punishing
one of my officers,

612
00:40:03,134 --> 00:40:09,606
I taught them all a
very important lesson.

613
00:40:09,674 --> 00:40:14,777
If they saw fit to call me
names after that, so be it.

614
00:40:14,846 --> 00:40:15,846
Hm.

615
00:40:17,082 --> 00:40:20,683
Then there was the, uh,
Officer Parsons incident.

616
00:40:20,752 --> 00:40:21,752
Your Honor,

617
00:40:21,820 --> 00:40:23,597
I still fail to see
the relevance.

618
00:40:23,621 --> 00:40:24,832
Mr. Matlock,

619
00:40:24,856 --> 00:40:27,890
as Captain Hanna
himself has just stated,

620
00:40:27,959 --> 00:40:31,361
his actions may
have been extreme,

621
00:40:31,429 --> 00:40:34,697
but they were not
necessarily irrational.

622
00:40:34,766 --> 00:40:38,201
And they hardly justify
the very serious allegations

623
00:40:38,269 --> 00:40:39,768
you seem to be making.

624
00:40:39,837 --> 00:40:45,074
Not by themselves, Your
Honor, no, probably not.

625
00:40:45,143 --> 00:40:47,877
But Jim Shanley wrote
down something else.

626
00:40:49,114 --> 00:40:52,181
At this time, I would like

627
00:40:52,250 --> 00:40:56,952
the last page of these notes
to be entered into evidence

628
00:40:57,022 --> 00:41:01,357
as Defense Exhibit B.

629
00:41:01,426 --> 00:41:04,894
I have made copies.

630
00:41:04,962 --> 00:41:11,867
It's a... a list of names: five.

631
00:41:11,937 --> 00:41:16,439
All five of these men
were involved in cases

632
00:41:16,507 --> 00:41:20,109
in which Captain
Hanna was also involved.

633
00:41:20,178 --> 00:41:23,479
They were all arrested
for one reason or another.

634
00:41:23,548 --> 00:41:27,550
And each was released
on a technicality,

635
00:41:27,619 --> 00:41:33,022
and each was found shot to death

636
00:41:33,091 --> 00:41:34,924
a short time later.

637
00:41:34,993 --> 00:41:36,692
Isn't that true, Captain Hanna?

638
00:41:36,761 --> 00:41:42,932
You knew that Jim Shanley
had figured it out, didn't you?

639
00:41:43,001 --> 00:41:47,336
The names... the
untraceable guns.

640
00:41:47,405 --> 00:41:49,638
What are you talking
about, Mr. Matlock?

641
00:41:49,707 --> 00:41:52,708
Didn't he tell you that he
didn't have enough proof

642
00:41:52,777 --> 00:41:54,077
to go to the D.A.,

643
00:41:54,145 --> 00:41:56,713
but he would keep digging
and digging until he did?

644
00:41:56,781 --> 00:42:00,516
This is total fabrication.

645
00:42:00,585 --> 00:42:03,152
You have unlimited access

646
00:42:03,221 --> 00:42:05,521
to the police property
room, don't you?

647
00:42:05,590 --> 00:42:06,767
Yes, I do.

648
00:42:06,791 --> 00:42:09,559
And the day after Jim
Shanley was murdered,

649
00:42:09,627 --> 00:42:12,996
a quarter pound of cocaine
turned up missing, didn't it?

650
00:42:13,064 --> 00:42:13,896
That's right.

651
00:42:13,965 --> 00:42:15,298
You recently sold your

652
00:42:15,366 --> 00:42:17,033
old pickup truck, didn't you?

653
00:42:17,102 --> 00:42:18,601
Well, yes, I did.

654
00:42:18,670 --> 00:42:20,670
For $5,000 cash?

655
00:42:20,739 --> 00:42:21,904
Of course, cash.

656
00:42:21,973 --> 00:42:23,305
And there's a master key

657
00:42:23,374 --> 00:42:25,552
to all the policemen's
lockers at the precinct,

658
00:42:25,576 --> 00:42:27,977
including Jerry
Reese, isn't that right?

659
00:42:28,046 --> 00:42:29,779
For emergency use, yes.

660
00:42:29,848 --> 00:42:31,592
And you know
where that key is kept!

661
00:42:31,616 --> 00:42:33,616
It's my job to know.

662
00:42:39,290 --> 00:42:43,793
Isn't this what really
happened, Captain Hanna?

663
00:42:45,730 --> 00:42:51,634
That night at the bar,
Jim Shanley told you

664
00:42:51,703 --> 00:42:54,270
as soon as he had
enough evidence,

665
00:42:54,338 --> 00:42:58,808
he was going to the Review
Board or Internal Affairs.

666
00:42:58,877 --> 00:43:02,278
An official inquiry.

667
00:43:02,346 --> 00:43:04,747
That could mean the
end of your career.

668
00:43:04,816 --> 00:43:08,818
Destroy your reputation.

669
00:43:08,887 --> 00:43:11,087
Place you in prison.

670
00:43:11,155 --> 00:43:14,690
Captain Edward
Hanna, the cop's cop.

671
00:43:16,761 --> 00:43:19,328
You got scared, didn't you?

672
00:43:19,397 --> 00:43:21,964
You went back to the precinct,

673
00:43:22,033 --> 00:43:25,868
and while Jerry Reese
was working out in the gym,

674
00:43:25,937 --> 00:43:28,204
you opened his locker

675
00:43:28,272 --> 00:43:31,741
and switched revolvers with him.

676
00:43:31,810 --> 00:43:33,842
Then you went to
Jim Shanley's home,

677
00:43:33,912 --> 00:43:37,213
forced him to call Jerry
Reese, told him to meet him

678
00:43:37,282 --> 00:43:42,584
at the Pike Street alley, and
there you killed Jim Shanley.

679
00:43:42,653 --> 00:43:44,787
And when Officer
Reese showed up,

680
00:43:44,856 --> 00:43:46,288
you choked him out,

681
00:43:46,357 --> 00:43:51,093
put the $5,000 in Jim Shanley's
pocket, planted the cocaine

682
00:43:51,162 --> 00:43:53,262
in Officer Reese's
car and took off.

683
00:43:56,000 --> 00:43:59,001
Ben... this is garbage.

684
00:43:59,070 --> 00:44:01,003
Right here.

685
00:44:02,974 --> 00:44:05,074
St. Jude medal.

686
00:44:05,142 --> 00:44:08,011
Your wife gave you one of these

687
00:44:08,079 --> 00:44:12,215
a year or two before
she died, didn't she?

688
00:44:12,283 --> 00:44:15,217
Yes.

689
00:44:15,286 --> 00:44:17,620
Was anything
inscribed on the back?

690
00:44:17,688 --> 00:44:19,322
Yes.

691
00:44:21,359 --> 00:44:25,494
"For luck forever.

692
00:44:25,563 --> 00:44:27,663
Love, Emily."

693
00:44:27,732 --> 00:44:34,103
Your men tell me you
wear that medal every day.

694
00:44:34,172 --> 00:44:37,640
Like it so much, you
gave each of them one.

695
00:44:37,708 --> 00:44:39,842
I wear it frequently,
but not every day.

696
00:44:39,910 --> 00:44:41,322
How about the night
Jim Shanley was killed?

697
00:44:41,346 --> 00:44:42,378
You wear it then?

698
00:44:42,447 --> 00:44:44,147
I don't remember.
Of course you did.

699
00:44:44,215 --> 00:44:48,251
Somehow, in his death struggle,

700
00:44:48,319 --> 00:44:50,353
he tore it from your neck.

701
00:44:50,421 --> 00:44:51,654
It fell to the ground.

702
00:44:51,722 --> 00:44:53,601
You probably scrambled
around looking for it.

703
00:44:53,625 --> 00:44:57,026
But then when
Officer Reese arrived,

704
00:44:57,094 --> 00:44:58,994
you had other work to do.

705
00:44:59,063 --> 00:45:02,064
And then when the third
car, the patrol car, arrived,

706
00:45:02,133 --> 00:45:05,334
you had to leave
without finding it,

707
00:45:05,403 --> 00:45:07,637
hoping no one else would.

708
00:45:07,705 --> 00:45:09,004
Am I right?

709
00:45:15,847 --> 00:45:17,679
Sorry, counselor.

710
00:45:17,748 --> 00:45:21,317
Oh... so you found it.

711
00:45:21,386 --> 00:45:23,085
It was never lost.

712
00:45:23,153 --> 00:45:29,692
Oh, but it was... right up until

713
00:45:29,760 --> 00:45:34,396
the moment you thought I
was going to go look for it.

714
00:45:34,465 --> 00:45:37,265
You had me detained
at the precinct

715
00:45:37,334 --> 00:45:40,803
while you went and
looked for it yourself.

716
00:45:42,773 --> 00:45:45,374
This is my medal,

717
00:45:45,442 --> 00:45:48,344
inscribed to me by my wife.

718
00:45:50,748 --> 00:45:52,748
Read the inscription.

719
00:46:12,937 --> 00:46:18,374
"For luck..." forever.

720
00:46:18,442 --> 00:46:20,742
Love, Emily."

721
00:46:20,811 --> 00:46:24,012
There's something else
inscribed on the medal, isn't there?

722
00:46:24,081 --> 00:46:28,617
At the very bottom,
in very small letters.

723
00:46:31,055 --> 00:46:35,824
Read the initials on
the bottom of the medal.

724
00:46:38,896 --> 00:46:46,101
The initials are... B.L.M.

725
00:46:46,170 --> 00:46:51,773
Benjamin Leighton Matlock.

726
00:46:51,842 --> 00:46:56,178
My initials.

727
00:47:00,117 --> 00:47:03,986
<i>We find the defendant,
Jerry Reese, not guilty.</i>

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
